    "input": "Explain fixed versus variable interest rates on a loan.",
    "response": "## Fixed vs. Variable Interest Rates on a Loan\n\n### **Fixed Interest Rate**\nA fixed rate remains constant throughout the entire term of your loan. Here's what you need to know:\n\n- **Predictable payments**: Your monthly principal and interest payment stays the same from start to finish.\n- **Loan terms**: Commonly used for mortgages, auto loans, and personal loans.\n- **No surprises**: You won't face unexpected increases if market conditions change.\n- **Typical use cases**: Long-term loans like mortgages where borrowers want budget certainty.\n\n**Example**: A $300,000 mortgage at a fixed 6% interest rate will have the same monthly payment regardless of whether inflation rises or falls over the life of the loan.\n\n### **Variable Interest Rate (Adjustable Rate)**\nA variable rate fluctuates based on a benchmark index (like SOFR, LIBOR, or prime rate) plus a margin set by the lender.\n\n- **Payment changes**: Your monthly payment may increase or decrease depending on the movement of the benchmark index.\n- **Initial advantage**: Often starts lower than a fixed-rate loan because the initial rate is tied to a low baseline.\n- **Risk**: Payments can go up significantly if the benchmark rises sharply.\n- **Common use cases**: Credit cards, some personal loans, and certain short-term lines of credit.\n\n**Example**: An adjustable-rate mortgage (ARM) might start at 5%, but if the benchmark index rises to 7%, your rate could jump to 7% (plus your lender's margin).\n\n---\n\n### **Key Differences at a Glance**\n\n| Feature | Fixed Rate | Variable Rate |\n|---------|------------|---------------|\n| **Rate stability** | Constant throughout | Changes periodically |\n| **Payment predictability** | Very predictable | Can vary |\n| **Upfront cost** | Usually higher (points/upfront fees) | Typically lower |\n| **Best for** | Budget certainty, long-term planning | Shorter commitment, potential for lower initial rates |\n\n### **Which Should You Choose?**\n- **Choose fixed** if you value predictability and want to lock in costs, especially for major purchases like homes.\n- **Choose variable** if you think rates will likely fall and want a potentially lower",
    "blocked": false,
    "layer": null,
    "latency_sec": 4.719
  },
